cartridge "CIRCUITS/ERIC HAINES/1989"

' CIRCUITS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Circuits" by Eric Haines, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette December 1989 (Commodore 64, machine language; author and
' rules from its title screen and its code, orig/circuits.dis). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a security computer has gone haywire. Pulses of electricity
' run along the wires of its circuit board; you cannot steer them, but you
' can throw the board's switches from your terminal. Route the pulses
' into the pyramids (power points): three hits destroy one, and a board is
' shut down when all its pyramids are gone. A pulse that runs into a
' burnt-out hole (black) or crosses a bridge set the other way crashes the
' circuit and costs a life.
'
' From the original (orig/): the five boards of 17 x 10 cells and the
' eight pulses' starts and directions, cell for cell; the cell rules: wires
' and corners, chips whose inside hides corners and reversers (the pulse
' is turned or sent back where nothing shows), shunts that turn a pulse at
' a T (two kinds, each thrown by its own stick direction), bridges that
' let pulses across one way only, transporter pads (a pulse entering one
' comes out of its partner, the same colour, going back the way it came;
' the stick cycles the colours of the second pads), pyramids hit three
' times (a pulse bounces back from a hit; a destroyed pyramid becomes a
' burnt-out hole), the original's turn tables ($1233, $1243); a cell's
' turn is taken at the cell's 5th of 16 steps, bridges and pyramids act
' on entering; pulses step every 50 passes of the C64 loop (about 21
' steps a second, here 7 steps every 20 frames) after a pause of 25 steps
' at each start; 3 lives a game, 3 safeties a board (FIRE: about 2.7
' seconds in which a crash only bounces the pulse back); the stick
' acts when it changes to a single direction (UP bridges, LEFT the
' left-hand shunts, DOWN the right-hand shunts, RIGHT the pads); points:
' a hit 10 x pulses, a pyramid 100 x pulses, a board 1000 x pulses, all
' five 10000 x pulses, then the boards again with one pulse more (to 8);
' 2 to 8 pulses chosen on the title (3 at first); 8-digit score and high
' score.
'
' Liberties: a cell is 8 x 16 pixels (17 cells do not fit 28 columns at
' 16 pixels); the pulses move half a pixel a step across and a pixel a step
' up and down, so a cell takes the same time both ways; new tiles (shunts
' show their way in yellow, bridges their closed way broken and dark,
' pads in four colours, the receiving pads with a hole); board 5's
' pyramid that no wire reaches (the published bug, fixed by readers with
' POKE 6437,0) is removed, so the board can be finished; the C64's sprite
' colours cycling about 19 times a second are a steady yellow (white
' while a safety is on); the joystick-port choice is gone (either
' controller plays); sounds and the title tune are new; a crash shows a
' spark and waits a second before the pulses start again.
'
' Controls: joystick only. UP throws the bridges, LEFT the left-hand
' shunts, DOWN the right-hand shunts, RIGHT cycles the pads' colours, a
' side button starts a safety. On the title, up and down choose, left and
' right change the number of pulses, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`.

